# The roots the telemetry uploader trusts, and the only ones.
#
# The ingest is served through Cloudflare, which issues its edge certificate
# from Let's Encrypt, Google Trust Services or SSL.com, and keeps a backup
# certificate from one of those or Sectigo that it deploys on its own if the
# primary is revoked. These are those four authorities' server roots. Every
# other root in the Mozilla set, including any authority later found
# compromised, cannot vouch for the ingest. Moving the ingest to an authority
# outside this list is an SDK release event (docs/telemetry.md).
#
# Where each certificate comes from, because not every one is in the Mozilla
# set:
# - Thirteen are byte-identical to the Mozilla root store entry of the same
#   name (webpki-roots 1.0.9) and to the Apple system root.
# - GTS Root R2 is byte-identical to the Apple system root and is one of the
#   four server roots Google Trust Services publishes, but the Mozilla set
#   does not carry it.
# - ISRG Root YE and ISRG Root YR are Let's Encrypt's Generation Y roots,
#   self-signed, from https://letsencrypt.org/certs/gen-y/. Let's Encrypt
#   issues from them (YE1, YR1) and today also serves cross-signs back to X2
#   and X1, which expire in 2032. They are pinned so that a chain which stops
#   at them still validates. No root program carries them yet.
#
# The SHA-256 fingerprints are pinned by the uploader's tests.

